# REDFISH PLUGIN - interaction with a BMC's Redfish service
#
#        2 defined methods are mandatory which return <True|False>,message:
#        - setting     modify properties of an existing resource (PATCH)
#        - upload      submit a resource, or invoke an action (POST)
#
#        2 optional methods:
#        - probe       read a resource and hand it back, where core wants to
#                      report what a BMC holds
#        - concepts    {concept: attribute name} for a board where a BIOS
#                      concept cannot be found through the registry's own
#                      DisplayNames; empty by default, filled on evidence only

# Plugin selection based on the following search path and priority:
#
# 1 node name                 e.g. plugins/redfish/node001.py
# 2 group name                e.g. plugins/redfish/compute.py
# 3 default                   e.g. plugins/redfish/default.py

# Core hands the plugin a client, it does not go and fetch one:
#
#        setting(redfish=<client>, uri=<resource>, payload=<decoded content>)
#
# The client is bound to one BMC and already carries its address and credentials,
# so a plugin never sees them and never has to know how we reach a BMC. Everything
# a plugin needs arrives as a parameter - it reads no database, writes no row and
# imports nothing from base.

# default.py is standards-based behaviour only, and that is deliberate. Where the
# Redfish service answers something - where a staged write belongs, when it
# applies, whether the work became a task - the service is the only source. A
# vendor file carries only what discovery genuinely cannot answer, and says for
# which model and firmware it was needed.
